   237	
   238	Task 1.1b answers the owner request "add a parity check after your rust engine,
   239	with kaggle environments". `scripts/kaggriculture_parity/generate_traces.py`
   240	runs `kaggle_environments.make("kaggriculture", configuration=...)` with an
   241	explicit seed and records traces in the official
   242	`kaggriculture-re-parity-v1` format: complete public and private state, statuses
   243	and rewards after every step. It refuses to run unless the installed package is
   244	1.32.7 and `envs/kaggriculture/kaggriculture.py` has exactly the SHA-256 pinned
   245	in `engine_rs/Cargo.toml`; each header records both. The project lock pins
   246	kaggle-environments 1.29.0, which has no Kaggriculture environment, so the
   247	generator runs in an isolated environment and the project lock is unchanged:
   248	
   249	```sh
   250	UV_OFFLINE=1 uv run --isolated --no-project --with kaggle-environments==1.32.7 \
   251	  python scripts/kaggriculture_parity/generate_traces.py --preset committed \
   252	  --out engine_rs/fixtures/generated --manifest
   253	```
   254	
   255	The format adds one record type. When Python's interpreter raises on an input,
   256	Kaggle's `env.step` fails and keeps its state; the generator records a
   257	`rejected` record (actions and Python exception) and then steps the policy's
   258	fallback actions. Rust must return an error for the same actions without
   259	changing any public or private state. Actions are recorded as submitted (after a
   260	JSON round trip); Kaggle's action-schema defaulting only adds missing top-level
   261	`farmer`/`hands`/`market` keys, which the interpreter defaults identically.
   262	
   263	Policies are seeded and selectable per seat: `random` (legal-looking unit
   264	commands for every actor, usually meaningful on the actor's tile, plus 0–4
   265	market orders and occasional over-limit queues); `edge` (the random policy plus
   266	HIRE until and past the 241-actor model capacity, 0/1023/negative/float/string/
   267	boolean/null/array/huge quantities, empty orders and markets, duplicate and
   268	reversed queues, BUY_LAND past three quadrants, unknown and lower-case verbs,
   269	malformed orders and unit commands, commands for non-existent hands,
   270	oversubscribed PLANT, malformed whole actions, and uncaught-`int()` probes);
   271	Kaggle's built-in `pass`, `random` (seeded through the policy RNG) and `starter`
   272	agents; and mixed seats. Configuration variants cover the defaults, free hires
   273	with 12 orders per turn (up to 249 hands, 250 actors), 10,000,000 starting money
   274	whose million-unit orders reach Python's 100,000-iteration market-loop escape,
   275	and a custom set (7 turns per day, capacity 30, weed chance 0.2, shop and
   276	town-centre intervals, hire multiplier 3, 4 orders and sparse `marketParams`
   277	overrides, including `hinge` and `log10` curves). Seeds include negative and
   278	above-`u64` integers. The 303 minimal probes step a two-turn preamble (buy
   279	wheat, a goose and carrots, hire, pick up), then one probe command: every
   280	malformed unit and order, 33 quantity spellings for PICKUP, PLACE and each market
   281	verb, unhashable verbs and items, missing-hand commands and malformed actions.
   282	
   283	`engine_rs/tests/replay_parity.rs` uses one comparator for three sources and
   284	reports the first divergence as line, step, kind and field path with expected
   285	and actual values. Rust starts from configuration and seed only.
   286	
   287	| Source | Traces | Transitions | Result |
   288	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   289	| Official recorded episodes | 4 | 2,876 | agree |
   290	| Committed generated games | 8 | 3,960 (25 Python rejections) | agree |
   291	| Committed divergence repros | 7 | 35 (5 Python rejections) | expected failures, asserted exactly |
   292	| Local sweep, 40 games | 40 | 21,824 (155 Python rejections) | agree |
   293	| Local sweep, 303 probes | 303 | 1,515 | 266 agree, 37 diverge (D1 12, D2 25) |
   294	
   295	The committed games are `random` vs `random`, `edge` vs `edge`, built-in
   296	`starter` vs built-in `random`, `random` vs `edge`, `edge` free-hire, `edge` vs
   297	`random` rich, `random` vs built-in `starter` custom, and built-in `pass` vs
   298	`edge` with seed −123,456; seeds are 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 2^64+7 and −123,456.
   299	`engine_rs/fixtures/generated/MANIFEST.json` pins each trace's SHA-256, size,
   300	policies, policy seed, configuration, counts and any expected divergence. The
   301	15 files total 667,059 bytes (budget 4,000,000). `scripts/check_engine_trim.py` validates
   302	that manifest, its exact file inventory, the Cargo engine pin and the budget;
   303	`TRIM_MANIFEST.json` pins the manifest bytes. Rust tests also fail when a
   304	committed state value, private key order, action or rejection claim is
   305	perturbed. A Python test regenerates the committed set from the live engine and
   306	requires identical bytes; it skips with a message when an isolated 1.32.7
   307	environment cannot be built offline.
   308	
   309	The recorded sweep ran `uv run python scripts/kaggriculture_parity/sweep.py
   310	--games 40` at commit `8ca378b` on the owner's Mac (single process, 46 s):
   311	base seed 20,260,929, 12 rotating policy/configuration pairs, 343 traces and
   312	23,339 transitions. Its summary, including per-policy counts and every first
   313	divergence, is `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/sweep-summary.json`. Set
   314	`KAGG_PARITY_TRACES=<absolute dir>` (and optionally `KAGG_PARITY_REPORT`) to
   315	replay a larger directory with the same comparator.
   316	
   317	#### Known Divergences
   318	
   319	Both classes concern malformed input only. The vendored kernel bytes are pinned,
   320	so they are recorded, not repaired; seven minimized repros are expected-failure
   321	fixtures with these reasons, and the test fails if any stops diverging exactly.
   322	Full-game policies exclude these inputs by default so a game can test the rest
   323	of the episode; `--include-known-divergences` restores them (12 such games: 8
   324	diverge, all classified D1 or D2).
   325	
   326	- **D1, Unicode decimal digits.** Python `int()` accepts non-ASCII decimal digit
   327	  strings such as `"\u0663"` (Arabic-Indic three) and `"\uff13"` (full-width
   328	  three). In PICKUP/PLACE counts Rust returns an error where Python acts; in
   329	  BUY_SEED, SELL, BUY_PRODUCT and BUY_ANIMAL quantities Rust drops the order
   330	  where Python executes three units. First found at step 13 of the free-hire
   331	  edge game (`BUY_PRODUCT FERTILIZER "\u0663"`, money 2,159 versus 2,460).
   332	- **D2, unhashable arrays/objects.** Python raises `TypeError`, so Kaggle's step
   333	  fails, when an array or object reaches a dict lookup: a unit verb
   334	  (`[["NORTH"]]`), a PLANT crop (also for a non-existent hand, via the atomic
   335	  seed check), a PICKUP or PLACE item, or a BUY_SEED or BUY_ANIMAL item. Rust
   336	  treats these as no-ops and accepts the step.
   337	
   338	All ASCII quantity spellings agree, including `" 4 "`, `"+2"`, `"0002"`,
   339	`"1_0"`, floats, booleans, `null`, arrays, 10^30 and uncaught-`int()` errors
   340	(`"abc"`, `null`, `[1]` counts), which both engines reject. Our policy's
   341	grammar emits only ASCII verbs, item names and integers, so neither class is
   342	reachable from model actions; an adapter accepting external actions would need
   343	explicit handling. The original failing full-game traces, their first
   344	divergences and the replay receipt are in
   345	`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/evidence/first-failing-games/`.
   346	
   347	Limits: generated games come from seeded random, edge and built-in policies,
   348	not strong play, and most random seats go bankrupt; the four official episodes
   349	remain the only recorded competitive worlds. Framework behavior outside the
   350	interpreter (timeouts, agent errors, `INVALID` statuses) is not modeled. The
   351	sweep is a bounded sample: 40 games and 303 probes, not exhaustive input
   352	coverage. A larger pod sweep remains open.

